Yes—as a transport and staging layer, you can absolutely use it, provided you sync exported SQL/JSON delta files rather than raw database data files.
In the Wintersys Agile Deployment Toolkit, the Heavyweight File System Syncing engine is already built to collate outgoing local filesystem additions/updates, push them through an S3-compatible datastore across regions, pull them down onto every machine in the federation, and handle housekeeping. [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Operations/FileSystemSyncing), [2] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Operations/Datastore%20Operational%20Protocols)
However, how you wire your database deltas into that directory determines whether it works cleanly or corrupts your data.

1. What Works vs. What Will Break

• Never sync live MySQL data directories (/var/lib/mysql). File-level sync tools copy files asynchronously without database transaction locks. Syncing live .ibd or InnoDB redo files across nodes will corrupt the database engine.
• Do sync immutable delta batch files (.sql or .json). If each node writes its 1-minute database deltas out as timestamped, node-tagged files (e.g., delta_node1_1732245840.sql) into a synced directory, ADT's heavyweight sync will automatically distribute those files to all 4 regions. [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Operations/FileSystemSyncing), [2] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Operations/WebrootSyncingMechanism)

2. How to Adapt ADT Heavyweight Sync for 4-Way DB Deltas

Because ADT's Webroot Syncing Mechanism resolves file collisions using newest file timestamp wins, you must design your delta files so two nodes never write to the same filename: [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Operations/WebrootSyncingMechanism)
1. Generate Unique, Immutable Delta Files per Node:
Every minute, each node queries its local delta_outbox (or mysqlbinlog slice) and writes a uniquely named file into its local synced folder:text
/var/sync/db_deltas/node1_20261122032400_0001.sql
Use code with caution.
Because each node only writes files prefixed with its own node_id and timestamp, two nodes never overwrite each other's delta files in the S3 datastore.
2. Let ADT Distribute the Files Across All 4 Regions:
ADT's heavyweight sync picks up the new .sql file from Node 1, pushes it to the shared datastore via SyncToDatastore.sh, and pulls it down into /var/sync/db_deltas/ on Nodes 2, 3, and 4. [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Operations/Datastore%20Operational%20Protocols), [2] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Operations/FileSystemSyncing)
3. Run an Applier Worker on Each Node:
A lightweight POSIX cron/daemon on each node watches /var/sync/db_deltas/ for incoming files from other nodes (node2_*, node3_*, node4_*):
	• Tracks applied filenames in a local SQLite or MySQL applied_deltas table (so it never applies the same file twice).
	• Sorts unapplied files chronologically by timestamp.
	• Sets SET @is_sync_worker = 1; so local triggers ignore the incoming changes.
	• Executes the .sql file against its local Linode Managed MySQL database and records the filename as applied.
4. Handle Housekeeping Carefully:
ADT's heavyweight sync tracks deletions across the federation. Instead of deleting .sql files immediately after one node applies them (which could delete the file from the datastore before Node 4 finishes downloading it), let a cleanup job purge delta files older than 24 hours once all nodes have ingested them. [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Operations/FileSystemSyncing)

Key Caveats to Watch Out For

• Sync Latency: Because ADT's filesystem sync runs on a periodic audit cycle (polling/tar archiving via ${HOME}/runtime/webroot_audit) plus your 1-minute database export, expect 1 to 3 minutes of eventual consistency across your 4 databases. [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Operations/WebrootSyncingMechanism)
• Row-Level Conflicts Still Apply: Even though unique filenames prevent file collisions in ADT, two regions updating the same database row in the same minute still need the Last-Writer-Wins (updated_at_utc) and non-overlapping primary key (UUID or offset auto-increment) rules in the generated .sql statements.
Would you like a POSIX script that plugs directly into an ADT synced folder—exporting local MySQL outbox deltas to unique .sql files and safely applying incoming .sql files from the other 3 nodes?

Here is a complete POSIX shell (/bin/sh) solution designed to drop into a directory managed by the Wintersys ADT heavyweight filesystem sync across your 4 nodes.
It splits the job into two clean responsibilities:
1. export_node_deltas.sh: Runs every minute on each node, drains a batch from that node's delta_outbox, and writes an immutable .sql file named <node_id>_<utc_timestamp>_<seq>.sql into the ADT-synced folder (using an atomic temp-file move so ADT never reads a half-written file).
2. apply_peer_deltas.sh: Runs every minute on each node, scans the synced folder for .sql files from other nodes (node_id != LOCAL_NODE_ID), applies them in chronological order inside a transaction with @is_sync_worker = 1, and records applied filenames in a local MySQL applied_delta_files table so files are never re-applied and can safely age out after 24 hours.

1. Ledger Table for Idempotent File Replay (All 4 Nodes)

Run this on each local MySQL database so every node remembers which peer delta files it has already applied:
sql
CREATE TABLE applied_delta_files (
    filename VARCHAR(255) PRIMARY KEY,
    source_node VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,
    applied_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);
Use code with caution.

2. Exporter Script (export_node_deltas.sh)

Each node writes files prefixed with its own NODE_ID (e.g., node1, node2, node3, node4) into SYNC_DIR. Because filenames include NODE_ID and timestamp, nodes never overwrite each other's files in the ADT datastore.
Save as /usr/local/bin/export_node_deltas.sh and chmod +x:
sh
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

# Configuration per node
NODE_ID="node1"                       # Unique per node: node1, node2, node3, node4
MYSQL_CNF="$HOME/.my-local.cnf"         # Connects to this node's Linode Managed MySQL
DB_NAME="defaultdb"
SYNC_DIR="/var/sync/db_deltas"          # Directory synced across nodes by ADT heavyweight sync
BATCH_SIZE=5000

LOCK_DIR="/tmp/adt_delta_export_${NODE_ID}.lock"
TMP_DIR=$(mktemp -d)
RAW_SQL="$TMP_DIR/batch.sql"
IDS_FILE="$TMP_DIR/ids.txt"

cleanup() {
    rm -rf "$TMP_DIR"
    rmdir "$LOCK_DIR" 2>/dev/null || true
}
trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM

# Prevent overlapping exports on this node
if ! mkdir "$LOCK_DIR" 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") [$NODE_ID] Export already running. Exiting."
    exit 0
fi

mkdir -p "$SYNC_DIR"

# 1. Fetch oldest outbox rows and render idempotent SQL statements
# Uses MySQL 8.0+ JSON_TABLE to unpack row_data cleanly.
# Adjust column list/assignments to match your table schema.
mysql --defaults-extra-file="$MYSQL_CNF" -D "$DB_NAME" --batch --raw --skip-column-names <<EOF > "$RAW_SQL"
SET @batch_limit = $BATCH_SIZE;

WITH batch AS (
    SELECT event_id, table_name, operation, row_id, row_data
    FROM delta_outbox
    ORDER BY event_id ASC
    LIMIT $BATCH_SIZE
)
SELECT CONCAT(
    '-- event_id=', b.event_id, ' table=', b.table_name, ' op=', b.operation, '\n',
    CASE
        WHEN b.table_name = 'orders' AND b.operation IN ('INSERT', 'UPDATE') THEN
            CONCAT(
                'INSERT INTO orders (id, customer_id, total, status, updated_at_utc, origin_node_id) VALUES (',
                JSON_EXTRACT(b.row_data, '$.id'), ', ',
                JSON_EXTRACT(b.row_data, '$.customer_id'), ', ',
                JSON_EXTRACT(b.row_data, '$.total'), ', ',
                QUOTE(JSON_UNQUOTE(JSON_EXTRACT(b.row_data, '$.status'))), ', ',
                QUOTE(JSON_UNQUOTE(JSON_EXTRACT(b.row_data, '$.updated_at_utc'))), ', ',
                QUOTE('$NODE_ID'),
                ') ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE ',
                'customer_id = IF(VALUES(updated_at_utc) > updated_at_utc OR (VALUES(updated_at_utc) = updated_at_utc AND VALUES(origin_node_id) > origin_node_id), VALUES(customer_id), customer_id), ',
                'total = IF(VALUES(updated_at_utc) > updated_at_utc OR (VALUES(updated_at_utc) = updated_at_utc AND VALUES(origin_node_id) > origin_node_id), VALUES(total), total), ',
                'status = IF(VALUES(updated_at_utc) > updated_at_utc OR (VALUES(updated_at_utc) = updated_at_utc AND VALUES(origin_node_id) > origin_node_id), VALUES(status), status), ',
                'origin_node_id = IF(VALUES(updated_at_utc) > updated_at_utc OR (VALUES(updated_at_utc) = updated_at_utc AND VALUES(origin_node_id) > origin_node_id), VALUES(origin_node_id), origin_node_id), ',
                'updated_at_utc = GREATEST(updated_at_utc, VALUES(updated_at_utc));'
            )
        WHEN b.table_name = 'orders' AND b.operation = 'DELETE' THEN
            CONCAT('DELETE FROM orders WHERE id = ', QUOTE(b.row_id), ';')
        ELSE ''
    END
) AS sql_stmt
FROM batch b
ORDER BY b.event_id ASC;

SELECT GROUP_CONCAT(event_id ORDER BY event_id ASC SEPARATOR ',')
FROM (
    SELECT event_id
    FROM delta_outbox
    ORDER BY event_id ASC
    LIMIT $BATCH_SIZE
) AS ids_sub;
EOF

# Separate SQL body and trailing IDs line (last non-empty line)
awk '
    NF { lines[++n] = $0 }
    END {
        if (n < 2) exit 1;
        for (i = 1; i < n; i++) print lines[i];
        print lines[n] > "'"$IDS_FILE"'";
    }
' "$RAW_SQL" > "$TMP_DIR/statements.sql" 2>/dev/null || {
    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") [$NODE_ID] No pending outbox deltas."
    exit 0
}

EVENT_IDS=$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$IDS_FILE")
if [ -z "$EVENT_IDS" ] || [ "$EVENT_IDS" = "NULL" ]; then
    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") [$NODE_ID] No pending outbox deltas."
    exit 0
fi

# 2. Build immutable filename and write atomically into SYNC_DIR
STAMP=$(date -u +"%Y%m%d%H%M%S")
FINAL_NAME="${NODE_ID}_${STAMP}_$$.sql"
STAGING_FILE="$TMP_DIR/$FINAL_NAME"
FINAL_PATH="$SYNC_DIR/$FINAL_NAME"

{
    printf -- "-- origin_node: %s\n" "$NODE_ID"
    printf -- "-- generated_at: %s\n" "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")"
    printf -- "SET @is_sync_worker = 1;\nSTART TRANSACTION;\n"
    cat "$TMP_DIR/statements.sql"
    printf -- "\nCOMMIT;\n"
} > "$STAGING_FILE"

# Move into synced directory only after file is completely written
mv "$STAGING_FILE" "$FINAL_PATH"

# 3. Remove exported events from local outbox
mysql --defaults-extra-file="$MYSQL_CNF" -D "$DB_NAME" -e \
    "DELETE FROM delta_outbox WHERE event_id IN ($EVENT_IDS);"

echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") [$NODE_ID] Exported $FINAL_NAME (events: $EVENT_IDS)."
Use code with caution.

3. Applier Script (apply_peer_deltas.sh)

Each node runs this script every minute to pick up .sql files from the other 3 nodes (node[1-4]_*.sql excluding its own NODE_ID), apply them in filename/timestamp order, and prune old files after 24 hours.
Save as /usr/local/bin/apply_peer_deltas.sh and chmod +x:
sh
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

# Configuration per node
NODE_ID="node1"                       # Must match this node's ID
MYSQL_CNF="$HOME/.my-local.cnf"
DB_NAME="defaultdb"
SYNC_DIR="/var/sync/db_deltas"
RETENTION_MINUTES=1440                # 24 hours before pruning old delta files

LOCK_DIR="/tmp/adt_delta_apply_${NODE_ID}.lock"
TMP_DIR=$(mktemp -d)
CANDIDATES_FILE="$TMP_DIR/candidates.txt"

cleanup() {
    rm -rf "$TMP_DIR"
    rmdir "$LOCK_DIR" 2>/dev/null || true
}
trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM

if ! mkdir "$LOCK_DIR" 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") [$NODE_ID] Applier already running. Exiting."
    exit 0
fi

[ -d "$SYNC_DIR" ] || exit 0

# 1. List peer .sql files (exclude own NODE_ID_*), sorted chronologically by name
find "$SYNC_DIR" -maxdepth 1 -type f -name "node*_[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]_[0-9]*.sql" \
    ! -name "${NODE_ID}_*" | sort > "$CANDIDATES_FILE"

while IFS= read -r filepath; do
    [ -n "$filepath" ] || continue
    fname=$(basename "$filepath")
    peer_node=$(printf "%s" "$fname" | awk -F'_' '{print $1}')

    # Escaped filename for SQL lookup/insert
    sql_fname=$(printf "%s" "$fname" | sed "s/'/''/g")
    sql_peer=$(printf "%s" "$peer_node" | sed "s/'/''/g")

    # 2. Skip if already applied
    already=$(mysql --defaults-extra-file="$MYSQL_CNF" -D "$DB_NAME" -N --batch --skip-column-names -e \
        "SELECT 1 FROM applied_delta_files WHERE filename = '$sql_fname' LIMIT 1;")

    if [ "$already" = "1" ]; then
        continue
    fi

    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") [$NODE_ID] Applying $fname from $peer_node..."

    # 3. Apply SQL file and record in applied_delta_files
    if mysql --defaults-extra-file="$MYSQL_CNF" -D "$DB_NAME" < "$filepath" && \
       mysql --defaults-extra-file="$MYSQL_CNF" -D "$DB_NAME" -e \
           "INSERT INTO applied_delta_files (filename, source_node) VALUES ('$sql_fname', '$sql_peer');"; then
        echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") [$NODE_ID] Applied $fname cleanly."
    else
        echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") [$NODE_ID] ERROR applying $fname. Stopping batch to preserve order." >&2
        exit 1
    fi
done < "$CANDIDATES_FILE"

# 4. Prune delta files older than 24h after all nodes have had ample time to ingest
find "$SYNC_DIR" -maxdepth 1 -type f -name "node*_[0-9]*.sql" -mmin "+$RETENTION_MINUTES" -delete

exit 0
Use code with caution.

4. Cron Schedule & ADT Integration Notes

Add both jobs to each node's crontab -e:
cron
* * * * * /usr/local/bin/export_node_deltas.sh >> /var/log/adt_delta_sync.log 2>&1
* * * * * /usr/local/bin/apply_peer_deltas.sh >> /var/log/adt_delta_sync.log 2>&1
Use code with caution.
• Why apply_peer_deltas.sh stops on error (exit 1): If node2_20261122032500_101.sql fails due to a transient lock or missing prerequisite, stopping the loop prevents node2_20261122032600_102.sql from running out of sequence behind it.
• Why files aren't deleted immediately on apply: Because ADT synchronises files across a federation, deleting a peer's file locally right after applying could cause ADT's deletion/sync cycle to race with another node still pulling from the datastore. Retaining files for 24 hours (-mmin +1440) gives every node plenty of window to converge before cleanup.
